      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The Toronto Maple Leafs have pivoted from a quiet 78-point finish - 8th in the Atlantic, season over - to a bold, high-stakes overhaul that begins with the net. <strong>Sergei Bobrovsky, the two-time Stanley Cup champion, has agreed to a three-year, $21M deal</strong>, instantly becoming the team's new starting goaltender and the centerpiece of GM John Chayka's aggressive free agency blitz. While the Leafs missed the playoffs last season, Chayka cleared cap space by trading Joseph Woll and others, betting that Bobrovsky's pedigree can resurrect a franchise that's been stagnant at the crease. The 37-year-old's career-worst recent save percentage raises eyebrows, yet his pedigree and the Leafs' urgent need for stability make this a calculated risk. <strong>What to watch now: how the front office reshapes the roster around Bobrovsky as free agency unfolds</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated July 2, 2026"
      },
