      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Toronto Blue Jays didn't just wake up on Canada Day; they roared back, turning a sluggish stretch into a statement. <strong>Guerrero Jr. showed real signs of progress in a 9-3 rout of the Mets</strong>, knocking three runs and breaking a quiet spell that had dogged him for weeks. The Jays sit at 41-46, third in the AL East, still 11 games behind the division lead, but that one-game win streak after a 2-1 victory and a 0-3 loss suggests a team finally finding its rhythm. This isn't contender energy yet, but it's honest momentum - especially with Guerrero, the heartbeat of the lineup, looking like the player we know he can be. The bullpen held firm, the bats connected, and for the first time in weeks, the Jays played like a team that believes it can climb. <strong>Next up: a Saturday night showdown at Seattle against the Mariners, where another win could push them closer to the top of the hunt.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated July 2, 2026"
      },
